 ## Overview
-Semantic Shader Augmentation is a technique that automatically extends an input GPU shader program with rule-based rendering functionality, replacing static visualization mappings (such as fixed transfer functions) with dynamic mappings defined by semantic rules and fuzzy membership functions [S1]. It solves the problem of rigid, hard-coded semantic layers by allowing arbitrary existing shaders to be dynamically augmented with the capability to evaluate illustration rules, enabling domain experts to interactively explore semantic mappings without programming skills [S1]. Unlike the original semantic-layers approach where rule-based functionality is hard-coded a priori, or UberShader/SuperShader which combine pre-coded fragments, this method generates code fragments entirely on the fly, allowing minimal overhead and quick exploration of different semantic mappings for various visualization techniques [S1].
+Semantic Shader Augmentation is a technique that automatically extends an input GPU shader program with rule-based rendering functionality, replacing static visualization mappings (such as fixed transfer functions) with dynamic mappings defined by semantic rules and fuzzy membership functions [S1]. It solves the problem of rigid, hard-coded visualization pipelines by allowing domain experts to interactively explore and specify multi-dimensional mappings without programming skills [S1]. Unlike the original semantic-layers approach where rule-based functionality is hard-coded a priori, or UberShader/SuperShader which combine pre-coded fragments, this method generates code fragments entirely on the fly, allowing the augmentation of any existing shader with minimal overhead [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates as a pre-compilation and dynamic loading pipeline. It takes an initial shader program (e.g., GLSL) containing special tags and a set of semantic rules, and produces an augmented shader program that can be dynamically loaded into the GPU [S1].
+The method operates by parsing an input shader source file, detecting specific tags that define variables as semantic entities, and injecting generated code to evaluate fuzzy visualization rules. The process involves two main automated steps: rendering semantic data properties to textures (Step 1) and enhancing the shader to evaluate a semantic rule base (Step 2) [S1].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input:** An initial shader source file (e.g., GLSL) selected by the user. This file contains the core rendering logic and special tags surrounding variables of potential interest [S1].
-*   **Input:** A set of semantic rules and membership functions, typically specified via a graphical user interface (GUI) [S1].
-*   **Output:** An augmented shader program (source code string) that encodes the intended semantic mapping and is loaded dynamically into the GPU context [S1].
+*   **Input Shader Program:** An initial shader program (e.g., GLSL) selected by the user. It may contain special tags surrounding variables of potential interest [S1].
+*   **Semantic Tags:** Dedicated tags in the shader code containing three pieces of information: a specifier, the name, and the type of the associated variable. These tags identify variables as either semantic data properties (inputs) or visual attributes (outputs) [S1].
+*   **Compositing Method:** Selectable by the user from averaging, maximum, or slicing [S1].
+*   **Mapping Factor:** A user-adjustable scalar factor multiplied with the composite value to map it to a visible data range [S1].
+*   **Membership Functions:** Defined via a graphical user interface using analogy. Implemented as single-channel textures where the dimensionality matches the number of dimensions of the represented data properties (e.g., 1D texture for 1D attribute) [S1].
 
